body:
Follow-up after running this change in production for a week, in a channel where several bots talk to each other.

`_self_is_explicitly_mentioned` now accepts the managed role, but `_self_is_raw_mentioned` (the inline-token check behind the bot admission gate, `DISCORD_ALLOW_BOTS=mentions` with `DISCORD_BOTS_REQUIRE_INLINE_MENTION`) still only looks for a literal `<@bot>`. When another bot types `@bot` and Discord resolves it to the managed role (`<@&role>`), the message passes the mention check and is then rejected by the inline gate, so bot-to-bot handoffs by role mention are dropped while the same mention from a human works.

A small addition on top of this PR's `_self_managed_role_mention_ids` helper fixes it:

```python
    def _self_is_raw_mentioned(self, message: Any) -> bool:
        """True only for a literal mention of this bot or its managed role.

        Reply-pings add us to ``message.mentions`` without an inline token, so
        the bot admission gate must check the message content itself.
        """
        if not self._client or not self._client.user:
            return False
        if str(self._client.user.id) in self._raw_mentioned_user_ids(message):
            return True
        content = str(getattr(message, "content", "") or "")
        return any(
            f"<@&{role_id}>" in content
            for role_id in self._self_managed_role_mention_ids(message)
        )
```

